    whatMakesItFamous:
      '*The Handmaid’s Tale* has become the most widely recognized dystopian novel of the last half century, and its central image has escaped the covers of the book entirely. Women in red robes and white winged bonnets now stand silently outside legislatures and courthouses around the world, wearing a costume borrowed from Atwood’s Republic of Gilead and understood instantly by people who have never read a page of the novel. Few books can claim to have supplied the visual vocabulary of a political movement, and fewer still have done it with a garment.\n\nThe book’s authority comes from a rule Atwood set herself while writing it: she would include nothing that some human society had not already done. Forced reproduction, public executions, book bans, the sudden freezing of women’s bank accounts, the seizure of children from mothers judged unfit, all of it has a documented ancestor somewhere in the historical record. Readers find Gilead terrifying not because it invents new cruelties but because it recombines familiar ones, and because Offred still remembers the ordinary world of laundromats and jogging shoes that came before.\n\nIts fame rests just as much on how the story is told. Offred narrates in a wary present tense, hoarding words the way she hoards a smuggled pat of butter for her skin, and she keeps reminding the reader that she is shaping a story rather than reporting a fact. The novel has been adapted into a film, an opera, a graphic novel, and an enormously successful television series, yet it is the voice on the page, watchful and dryly funny under pressure, that keeps sending readers back to the book itself.',
