        body:
          'Gilead is organized around a single scarcity: functioning wombs. Every institution in the novel, from the color-coded uniforms to the monthly Ceremony to the bodies of executed doctors displayed on the Wall, exists to manage reproduction and to place it under male authority. Handmaids are not people who happen to bear children; they are, as Offred puts it, two-legged wombs, sacred vessels valued for the one part of them that still works.\n\nAtwood keeps this abstraction physical. Lying in the bath, Offred looks at herself with the detachment of someone inspecting equipment, thinking of her body as a cloud congealed around a central object shaped like a pear, harder and more real than she is. Her name, her clothing, her food, her sleep, and her movements have all been rearranged around a monthly cycle she does not control and cannot refuse.\n\nWhat gives the theme its edge is that Gilead insists it is protecting women. Aunt Lydia teaches that the old world offered freedom to and the new one offers freedom from, and she can point at real dangers to justify the trade. The novel declines to make that argument a straw man, which is why the bargain at its center, safety in exchange for personhood, still reads as a live temptation rather than a historical curiosity.',
