      'Margaret Atwood was born in Ottawa in 1939 and spent much of her childhood in the northern Canadian bush, where her father worked as an entomologist. She has since published more than fifty books of poetry, fiction, and criticism, and she has been a central figure in Canadian letters since the late 1960s, when her early poetry and her critical writing on Canadian literature established her reputation.\n\nShe wrote *The Handmaid’s Tale* in West Berlin in the mid-1980s, while the city was still divided by the Wall, and she has often described how the atmosphere of a watched society fed the book. Her own family background supplied the other half of the material, since she is descended from New England Puritans and dedicated the novel in part to Mary Webster, a woman accused of witchcraft in seventeenth-century Massachusetts who survived a hanging. Atwood has said consistently that she invented none of Gilead’s practices and drew them all from the historical record.\n\nMore than thirty years later she returned to Gilead in *The Testaments*, published in 2019, which shared the Booker Prize and retold the world through three new voices, including that of Aunt Lydia. In the decades between the two books her most famous novel has only grown in prominence, invoked constantly in arguments over reproductive rights and adapted for film, opera, and television. Atwood continues to write and to speak publicly about politics and the environment, and she has watched a costume she invented become a form of protest in cities she never imagined while writing it.',
