          'In the Kabul of the early 1970s, before the coups and the invasions, Amir is the bookish son of a wealthy Pashtun businessman known as Baba, and Hassan is the son of Baba’s Hazara servant, Ali. The two boys are nursed by the same woman and grow up inseparable, carving their names into a pomegranate tree as the sultans of Kabul, yet the arrangement is never equal: Amir can read and Hassan cannot, and Amir sometimes uses that difference to be cruel.\n\nEverything turns on the kite tournament of the winter of 1975. Amir cuts the last kite from the sky and Hassan runs to retrieve the trophy, calling back over his shoulder that he would do it for Amir a thousand times over. When Amir finds him, Hassan is cornered in an alley by Assef, a sadistic older boy with brass knuckles, refusing to give up the blue kite. Amir watches from the shadows, decides not to intervene, and runs.\n\nHe cannot live beside the person who reminds him of his cowardice. Amir plants his birthday watch and a handful of cash under Hassan’s mattress and accuses him of stealing, and Hassan, understanding exactly what is happening, confesses to a theft he did not commit. Baba forgives him instantly, which wounds Amir further, but Ali insists that he and his son leave the household. The boys never see each other again.',
