    aboutAuthor:
      'Khaled Hosseini was born in Kabul in 1965, the eldest son of a diplomat in the Afghan Foreign Ministry and a teacher of Farsi and history. The family was posted abroad in the 1970s, and while they were out of the country the Soviet Union invaded Afghanistan, making return impossible. They were granted political asylum in the United States in 1980 and settled in northern California, where Hosseini learned English as a teenager and later trained as a physician.\n\nHe wrote *The Kite Runner* in the early mornings before hospital shifts, drawing on childhood memories of Kabul kite tournaments and on the history of invasion, civil war, and Taliban rule that had overtaken the city he left. The novel was published in 2003, sold slowly at first, and then became one of the defining bestsellers of the decade, which allowed him to give up medicine for writing.\n\nHosseini has since published further novels set in Afghanistan, including *A Thousand Splendid Suns*, which turns to the lives of Afghan women. He has also worked as a goodwill envoy for the United Nations refugee agency and established a foundation providing humanitarian assistance in Afghanistan. He has spoken often about the odd position of writing a country for readers who know it mainly through news coverage, and about his wish that his books restore ordinary life to a place usually described only in terms of its wars.',
