    aboutAuthor:
      'Marjane Satrapi was born in Rasht, Iran, in 1969 and grew up in Tehran in a progressive, highly educated family descended from one of the country’s former ruling dynasties. She was a child when the 1979 revolution overthrew the Shah, and a schoolgirl when the war with Iraq began. At fourteen her parents sent her alone to Vienna for her own safety, the decision that divides her memoir in half.\n\nAfter returning to Iran, studying art, and marrying and divorcing young, she left for Europe permanently in the mid-1990s and settled in France, where she trained as an illustrator and fell in with a group of Paris comics artists who encouraged her to tell her own story in the form. *Persepolis* appeared in French in a series of volumes beginning in 2000 and was later collected in English in two parts.\n\nSatrapi co-directed an animated adaptation of the memoir that was nominated for an Academy Award, and she has since worked mainly as a filmmaker and painter. She has spoken often about her motive for writing the book, which was to refuse the reduction of an entire nation to the image of its government, and she has resisted being treated as a spokesperson for Iranian women. The memoir remains among the most assigned and most frequently challenged books in American schools.',
