        body:
          'The prose is built for quotation. Sentences like the promise that the universe conspires to help a person who truly wants something are constructed to be lifted out and repeated, and the book is full of similar formulations about hearts, fear, and beginner’s luck. Coelho repeats his key phrases at intervals so that they accumulate the authority of proverbs.\n\nThe vocabulary is deliberately plain, and the translation has kept it plain, which is part of why the novel travels so well across languages and reading levels. A confident twelve-year-old and an adult in the middle of a career change can read the same pages and take away something usable, which very few books manage.\n\nThat accessibility is precisely what critics object to, arguing that the aphorisms substitute reassurance for thought and that a maxim repeated often enough begins to feel like wisdom without ever having been tested. The disagreement is genuine and unresolved, and it makes the book a useful case for a class discussion about the difference between a sentence that sounds true and a sentence that is.',
